## Memory Leaks

Memory leaks are a common problem in C and C++. Memory leaks are a problem that occurs when a program does not free the memory that it allocated. Memory leaks can cause a program to run out of memory and crash.

To find memory leaks in C and C++, we can use the `valgrind` tool. `valgrind` is a memory leak detector that can be used to find memory leaks in C and C++.

To use `valgrind`, we need to compile the program with debugging symbols enabled. To do this, we can use the `-g` flag when compiling the program.

```bash
gcc -g program.c -o program
```

Next, we can run `valgrind` on the program to find memory leaks.

```bash
valgrind./program
```

`valgrind` will run the program and find memory leaks. If there are memory leaks, `valgrind` will print a message that the program has detected a memory leak.

To fix memory leaks, we can use the `free()` function to free the memory that was allocated by the `malloc()` function.

